            <ArticleTitle>Baseline heart rate-adjusted electrocardiographic triggering for coronary artery electron-beam CT angiography.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>590-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Conventional electrocardiographic (ECG) triggering (group 1, 53 patients) was compared with baseline heart rate-adjusted ECG triggering (group 2, 54 patients) for coronary artery electron-beam computed tomographic (CT) angiography. CT angiographic data sets were compared blindly with conventional angiograms according to segment. Nonassessability of coronary artery segments was reduced from 35% in group 1 to 13% in group 2 (P &lt; .001). More motion-free coronary artery images were obtained in group 2 than in group 1, especially in the right coronary artery (95% vs 67%, P &lt; .001). Overall sensitivity and specificity for luminal stenosis (&gt; or =50%) were 69% and 82% (group 1) and 76% and 92% (group 2) (P &gt; .05 and P &lt; .001, respectively). Baseline heart rate-adjusted ECG triggering improves image quality at coronary artery CT angiography for detection of coronary artery disease.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
